Interview Question 1 — User Prompt vs System Prompt

Interviewer:

In your AI Project Planner, you have ANALYSIS_SYSTEM_PROMPT and you also send project information through contents. Why did you put the project-planning rules in the system 
prompt instead of putting them together with the project information? What would be the difference if you put everything into contents?



//=============

I separate them because they have different responsibilities.

The system prompt contains relatively stable application-level instructions and rules that define how the AI should behave. For example, my `ANALYSIS_SYSTEM_PROMPT` tells 
Gemini to act as a senior software architect and return the project overview and complexity in a specific structure.

The contents contain dynamic, request-specific project information such as the project name, description, technology stack, and deadline.

Keeping them separate makes the prompt easier to maintain, reuse, and control. It also clearly separates the AI's instructions from the data that the AI needs to process.

In my project, I use dynamic prompt construction with template literals to insert the user's project data. I use context injection specifically when I pass the output from one 
AI step into the next step of my multi-step pipeline.
